===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[dark]] or [[gloomy]].
#* {{RQ:Goldsmith Deserted Village|page=17|passage=Here, while the proud their long drawn pomps diſplay, / There the black gibbet '''glooms''' beſide the way.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1891|author=Charles Egbert Craddock [pseudonym; {{w|Mary Noailles Murfree}}]|title=In the "Stranger People's" Country|location=New York|publisher=w:Harper & Brothers|oclc=262470798|page=323
|passage=Around all the dark forest '''gloomed'''.|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/instrangerpeople00cradiala/page/323/mode/1up}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To look or feel [[sad]], [[sullen]] or [[despondent]].
#: {{syn|en|grieve|mourn|Thesaurus:be sad}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1882|W. Marshall|Strange Chapman: A North of England Story|volume=2|location=London|publisher=Hurst and Blackett|oclc=1085328201|page=170|passage=Her face gathers, furrows, '''glooms'''; arching eyebrows wrinkle into horizontals, and a tinge of bitterness unsmooths the cheek and robs the lip of sweetened grace. She is evidently perturbed.|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/strangechapmanno02mars/page/170/mode/1up}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1930|author=w:D. H. Lawrence|title=The Lovely Lady
|passage=Ciss was a big, dark-complexioned, pug-faced young woman who seemed to be '''glooming''' about something.}}
#* {{RQ:James Golden Bowl|volume=I|chapter=XVI|page=283|passage="Is Maggie then astonishing too?"—and he '''gloomed''' out of his window.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=85|passage=He '''gloomed''' for some moments above the round-topped table[.]}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To render gloomy or dark; to [[obscure]]; to [[darken]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Maud|page=115|passage=A black yew '''gloom'd''' the stagnant air.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fill with gloom; to make sad, dismal, or sullen.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|page=110|passage=For see you not, dear love, / Such a mood as that, which lately '''gloom'd''' / Your fancy when you saw me following you, / Must make me fear still more you are not mine, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Goldsmith Deserted Village|page=20|passage=Good Heaven! What ſorrows '''gloom'd''' that parting day, / That called them from their native walks away; {{...}}}}
# To shine or appear obscurely or imperfectly; to [[glimmer]].

